WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.100
Does an axon read an address in its own receptor level, or its rank among the

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.100
others?

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.440
In a normal animal, both readings put the axons in the same order.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.300
To tell them apart, change some axons but not their neighbours.

5
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.670
In 2000, Arthur Brown, Greg Lemke and colleagues built such a mouse.

6
00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:29.003
Isl2 is a gene switched on in about half of the retinal ganglion cells, scattered

7
00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:30.003
across the retina.

8
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:34.117
They inserted the receptor EphA3 into that gene.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:37.457
Mouse ganglion cells do not normally make it.

10
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.990
Now neighbours at every point in the retina differed.

11
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:45.483
Use the toy numbers.

12
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:50.343
The knock-in adds four units, so knock-in axons carry five to seven.

13
00:00:51.223 --> 00:00:53.243
Their unchanged neighbours carry one to three.

14
00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:56.977
First, the absolute reading, a fixed threshold.

15
00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:00.897
Where do the knock-in axons go, and their unchanged neighbours?

16
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:10.117
In the toy, the knock-in axons crowd toward the front.

17
00:01:10.957 --> 00:01:14.297
The unchanged axons keep exactly their normal places.

18
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.657
Now the relative reading, in its simplest form: pure rank.

19
00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:20.557
Where does each group go?

20
00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:29.837
Every knock-in axon outranks every unchanged one.

21
00:01:30.697 --> 00:01:34.657
So knock-in axons take the front half, and unchanged axons the back.

22
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:40.097
In mice with two copies of the knock-in, Brown and colleagues found two orderly

23
00:01:40.097 --> 00:01:41.097
maps.

24
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.207
Look at the unchanged axons.

25
00:01:44.207 --> 00:01:47.247
Nothing about them was altered, yet they ended somewhere new.

26
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.650
An absolute reading cannot produce that.

27
00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.250
Their place depended on the other axons.

28
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.443
So is the amount of ligand irrelevant?

29
00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:01.563
Try a thought test.

30
00:02:02.423 --> 00:02:04.803
Lower the ephrin-A everywhere, say by half.

31
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:08.987
What does each reading predict now?

32
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.430
The fixed threshold says the map shifts back.

33
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.150
Pure rank says nothing moves, because no rank has changed.

34
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.470
Real mice with one working copy of each of two ephrin-A genes kept a normal main

35
00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.470
zone.

36
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:34.673
But in some animals, temporal axons also formed an extra patch further back.

37
00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:37.297
Pure rank cannot give that.

38
00:02:38.097 --> 00:02:41.077
The amount of ligand affects how accurately axons map.

39
00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:46.087
So neither toy is the whole story.

40
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:49.087
In mouse and chick, the slope sets the scale.

41
00:02:50.407 --> 00:02:52.247
Comparison among axons sets who goes where.

42
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.110
Slopes help lay out the coarse map.

43
00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:00.050
Correlated activity in the retina helps sharpen it, partly at the same time.

44
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:01.930
That is the next unit.

45
00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:08.103
The takeaway: an axon's place is a rank, set against a slope.
